{"api":1,"id":"aerosols","engine":"sweep","units":"share of the thickest places of each aerosol","range":[0,1],"hotLine":0.5,"leftLon":-180,"words":{"subject":"planet","map":"aerosol map","dates":"moment","what":"aerosol","top":"thickest","missing":"not read."},"sweep":{"src":"data/03_aerosols.json","chapter":"still","mapping":"aerosols","instrument":"vibraphone"},"legend":{"summary":"NASA's GEOS model of the aerosols in the air on 19 August 2025 at 20:00 UTC: smoke, desert dust, sea salt and sulfate. A scan line crosses the world once, west to east, in about 35 seconds.\n","method":"Each aerosol is its own wind. More of it under the scan line makes its wind louder and brighter; where there is none, it falls silent. North sounds higher and in your left ear, south lower and in your right ear. Listen to all four winds together, or to one at a time. NASA's stories: instead of the whole map, hear the six events NASA describes on this map, one after another. The scan line crosses only a window around each event, more slowly. The wind of the aerosol NASA names for it is brought forward, and the others stay, a little softer. Inside a window, high and low and the two ears stretch to fit it.","items":[{"sound":"Black carbon: dark, rough wind","meaning":"Smoke from fires. A low, dark, slightly rough wind, with soft crackles where the smoke is thickest, as over central Africa. In NASA's smoke stories, a low cello plays where the smoke is thickest."},{"sound":"Dust: sandy wind","meaning":"Desert dust. A grainy wind in the middle, as over the Sahara and the dust blowing from it across the Atlantic. In NASA's dust story, a kalimba plays along the dust."},{"sound":"Sea salt: low ocean wind","meaning":"Salt spray from the sea. A low, smooth, airy wind, strongest over the stormy southern oceans. In the Hurricane Erin story, a harp traces the storm's spiral of sea spray."},{"sound":"Sulfate: thin, high wind","meaning":"Sulfate from industry, ships and volcanoes. A thin, high, breathy wind, very soft, as over South and East Asia and the North Pacific. In the volcano stories, a vibraphone rings along the stream of sulfate."},{"sound":"Higher or lower, left or right","meaning":"Where that aerosol is under the scan line: north is higher and in your left ear, south is lower and in your right ear. In NASA's six stories the line crosses only a window around each event, so high and low stretch to fit the window."}],"caveat":"Values are read from the colours of NASA's picture, not from the model's numbers. NASA's key has colours but no numbers, so each aerosol is measured against its own thickest places: a loud dust wind and a loud sulfate wind are not the same amount. Where layers overlap they were separated by colour, so a thin layer under a thick one can be missed, and sea salt is read only over the sea and near coasts. City lights and fire dots drawn on the map are not aerosol and were removed first. This is one moment, 19 August 2025 at 20:00 UTC, not an average.\n"},"layers":[{"id":"black-carbon","name":"Black carbon","key":"B","image":"media/03_aerosols/black-carbon.webp","colour":"#d25e45"},{"id":"dust","name":"Dust","key":"U","image":"media/03_aerosols/dust.webp","colour":"#c19adc"},{"id":"sea-salt","name":"Sea salt","key":"T","image":"media/03_aerosols/sea-salt.webp","colour":"#85aaa9"},{"id":"sulfate","name":"Sulfate","key":"L","image":"media/03_aerosols/sulfate.webp","colour":"#53dc34"}],"about":{"what":"Four kinds of tiny particles in the air at one moment: smoke (black carbon), dust, sea salt and sulfate, and six stories NASA tells about them.","who":"NASA's Global Modeling and Assimilation Office ran the GEOS model. Joseph V. Ardizzone and Helen-Nicole Kostis made the visualization, with scientist Lesley Ott.","when":"19 August 2025, 20:00 UTC: one moment, not an average.","where":"The whole world. NASA's stories are Hurricane Erin, dust from the Sahara, Kilauea, Etna, smoke over southern Africa and wildfires in Spain.","why":"To hear that the air is shared: smoke, dust and salt cross oceans and borders.","how":"Each aerosol is its own wind, louder where there is more of it. In NASA's stories the aerosol NASA names also plays its own instrument.","journey":[{"title":"Satellites, ground stations and weather balloons","detail":"About a million observations an hour feed the model."},{"title":"GEOS model","detail":"NASA's Goddard Earth Observing System."},{"title":"NASA Scientific Visualization Studio","detail":"SVS 5669, GEOS Aerosols."},{"title":"NASA Earth Information Center","detail":"The NASA exhibit and website whose videos and images this challenge starts from."},{"title":"Hearth","detail":"Reads NASA's pictures and plays them as sound in your browser. Nothing is uploaded."}]},"views":{"legend":"Mode","key":"R","options":[{"id":"stories","name":"NASA's six stories","unit":"story","frames":[{"when":"Hurricane Erin","text":"Hurricane Erin. The first Atlantic hurricane of 2025, off the east coast of the United States. Its strong winds whip sea spray into a spiral of sea salt, and it pulls in desert dust from Africa. Loudest winds here: sea salt, then sulfate.","box":[-82.5,19.5,-64.5,37.5],"focus":"sea-salt","base":0,"view":"hurricane-erin","frame":0},{"when":"Dust from the Sahara","text":"Dust from the Sahara. Dust from the Sahara blows west across the whole Atlantic Ocean and makes the sky hazy in the Caribbean, as far as the United States. The scan line starts at the Caribbean end and follows the dust back to the desert. Loudest winds here: dust, then sea salt, then sulfate.","box":[-85.5,7.5,34.5,36.0],"focus":"dust","base":0,"view":"saharan-dust","frame":0},{"when":"Kilauea volcano, Hawaii","text":"Kilauea volcano, Hawaii. Hawaii's Kilauea volcano gives off a narrow stream of sulfate that drifts west over the Pacific Ocean. The scan line starts at the far end of the stream and ends at the volcano. Loudest winds here: sulfate, then sea salt.","box":[-180.0,12.0,-153.0,28.5],"focus":"sulfate","base":0,"view":"kilauea","frame":0},{"when":"Mount Etna, Italy","text":"Mount Etna, Italy. Mount Etna, the volcano on the island of Sicily, sends streams of sulfate over southern Europe. Loudest winds here: sulfate.","box":[10.5,33.0,27.0,46.5],"focus":"sulfate","base":0,"view":"etna","frame":0},{"when":"Smoke over southern Africa","text":"Smoke over southern Africa. Farmers burn fields at this time of year in southern Africa, and the smoke drifts west over the Atlantic Ocean. Loudest winds here: black carbon, then sulfate.","box":[-15.0,-25.5,40.5,4.5],"focus":"black-carbon","base":0,"view":"southern-africa","frame":0},{"when":"Wildfires in Spain","text":"Wildfires in Spain. Wildfires burn in several parts of Spain, and their smoke is thickest over the north-west of the country. Loudest winds here: black carbon, then sulfate, then dust.","box":[-10.5,37.5,4.5,49.5],"focus":"black-carbon","base":0,"view":"spain","frame":0}]}]},"places":{"2,3":"Anchorage","2,15":"Reykjavík","3,5":"Prince George","3,6":"Calgary","3,17":"London","3,19":"Berlin","3,20":"Warsaw","3,21":"Moscow","3,26":"Novosibirsk","4,5":"Seattle","4,7":"Billings","4,8":"Winnipeg","4,9":"Chicago","4,10":"New York","4,17":"Madrid","4,18":"Paris","4,19":"Rome","4,20":"Istanbul","4,24":"Tashkent","4,25":"Almaty","4,28":"Ulaanbaatar","4,31":"Vladivostok","5,5":"San Francisco","5,6":"Los Angeles","5,7":"Denver","5,8":"Dallas","5,9":"Atlanta","5,10":"Washington","5,17":"Casablanca","5,18":"Algiers","5,21":"Cairo","5,22":"Baghdad","5,23":"Tehran","5,24":"Kabul","5,25":"Lahore","5,28":"Chengdu","5,29":"Beijing","5,30":"Shanghai","5,31":"Tokyo","6,2":"Honolulu","6,8":"Houston","6,9":"Miami","6,22":"Riyadh","6,23":"Dubai","6,24":"Karachi","6,25":"Delhi","6,26":"Kolkata","6,27":"Dhaka","6,29":"Guangzhou","6,30":"Taipei","7,8":"Mexico City","7,11":"Caracas","7,16":"Dakar","7,21":"Khartoum","7,25":"Mumbai","7,27":"Yangon","7,28":"Bangkok","7,30":"Manila","8,10":"Bogotá","8,17":"Accra","8,18":"Lagos","8,19":"Bangui","8,